1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,440
Proposition thirty-nine would add identification requirements each time

2
00:00:04,440 --> 00:00:09,580
Californians vote. Citizenship is already required for state and federal

3
00:00:09,580 --> 00:00:15,200
elections. The question is how identity and eligibility are checked, not

4
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:19,180
whether those elections should allow noncitizens to vote. Today,

5
00:00:19,180 --> 00:00:24,520
registration uses identifying information. Most in-person voters sign a

6
00:00:24,520 --> 00:00:29,560
roster; mail voters sign their ballot envelope, and election officials check

7
00:00:29,560 --> 00:00:34,100
that signature. The proposal would add government-identification checks

8
00:00:34,100 --> 00:00:35,640
before ballots are counted.

9
00:00:36,167 --> 00:00:41,507
Imagine a voter preparing a mail ballot at home. Under the proposal, that

10
00:00:41,507 --> 00:00:46,487
voter would select a government-issued identification in their registration,

11
00:00:46,487 --> 00:00:51,147
then write its last four digits on the ballot envelope. Officials would

12
00:00:51,147 --> 00:00:55,607
compare those digits with the registration record. An election worker helps

13
00:00:55,607 --> 00:01:00,957
explain the identification process. An in-person voter would instead present

14
00:01:00,957 --> 00:01:05,427
government-issued identification. The state must offer a free voter

15
00:01:05,427 --> 00:01:10,307
identification card on request. The Legislature would still have to clarify

16
00:01:10,307 --> 00:01:14,387
details, including which forms of identification qualify.

17
00:01:15,233 --> 00:01:18,793
Officials would make best efforts using government data to verify

18
00:01:18,793 --> 00:01:24,153
citizenship on voter rolls, publish annual verification percentages, and

19
00:01:24,153 --> 00:01:28,573
face state compliance audits every other year. Those checks are separate

20
00:01:28,573 --> 00:01:33,233
from presenting identification at the polling place. The analyst estimates

21
00:01:33,233 --> 00:01:37,973
annual state and local costs from tens of millions to the low hundreds of

22
00:01:37,973 --> 00:01:42,993
millions of dollars. Card demand, implementation rules, staffing, and

23
00:01:42,993 --> 00:01:47,473
technology choices affect the total. A free card still costs government

24
00:01:47,473 --> 00:01:52,593
money to provide. This is a proposed change, not instructions for casting

25
00:01:52,593 --> 00:01:56,853
your current ballot. For voting today, use the official election

26
00:01:56,853 --> 00:01:59,633
instructions and your county elections office.

27
00:02:00,200 --> 00:02:04,420
A voter might favor a consistent identification check as another

28
00:02:04,420 --> 00:02:09,200
verification layer and a way to strengthen confidence. The free-card

29
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:13,800
provision addresses the purchase cost of identification. Whether the

30
00:02:13,800 --> 00:02:17,880
additional checks deliver benefits proportionate to their costs is the

31
00:02:17,880 --> 00:02:18,560
question to weigh.

32
00:02:19,300 --> 00:02:23,860
You might ask what happens to eligible voters without documents. A skeptical

33
00:02:23,860 --> 00:02:28,880
voter could worry about administrative mistakes, obtaining documents, or

34
00:02:28,880 --> 00:02:34,360
navigating new rules. A free card does not remove every time or paperwork

35
00:02:34,360 --> 00:02:39,300
burden. Existing checks already operate, so opponents question the extra

36
00:02:39,300 --> 00:02:42,200
system’s necessity and its effect on access.

37
00:02:42,967 --> 00:02:48,567
In the September PPIC survey of likely voters, 43 percent said yes, 55

38
00:02:48,567 --> 00:02:54,267
percent no, and 1 percent were unsure. These are dated survey results, not

39
00:02:54,267 --> 00:03:00,227
votes already cast. A later September Berkeley survey found 39 percent yes,

40
00:03:00,227 --> 00:03:06,187
52 percent no, and 9 percent undecided. Question wording and methods differ,

41
00:03:06,187 --> 00:03:11,307
so comparing polls does not by itself establish a trend. The Independent

42
00:03:11,307 --> 00:03:17,027
Voter Project survey ending October second found 43 percent yes and 40

43
00:03:17,027 --> 00:03:21,947
percent no. Its methods and wording differ again. The disagreement is a

44
00:03:21,947 --> 00:03:25,187
